gness to discover and find in the  evil of that other person a human being  and the possibilities of good and the  recovery of good and an acknowledgment  that she as a person was not by  definition good  untainted perfect but also she could  have become evil or done wicked things  so it's not the destruction of evil  that's involved it's the transformation  of people who are evil even in this case  in conditions of restraint it wasn't in  that sense an equal meeting he was  trained at first he was locked up he was  disempowered he was deprived of the  possibilities of doing what he done  before perhaps even more important he  was stripped of the arrogance the moral  weapons that he'd had under apartheid  when he'd been congratulated and  rewarded when people flocked to the farm  to attend the barbecues to enjoy that  sense of triumph at exterminating what  they regarded as the evil enemy the  Freedom Fighters the resistance turning  the guerrillas around to become weapons  to fight other freedom fighters and that  world had been destroyed and collapsed  democracy had defeated it but not by  taking his life capital punishment  abolished in South Africa at the behest  of those who had suffered most from the  violence and the trauma from his ranks  as permanent said from her people from  our people the executed had been plucked  previously  it was a search for a new way of doing  things a new approach to humanizing a  country that had seen so much barbarity  and what was so striking and forceful  was that this search and eagerness to  reclaim the humanity even of the most  depraved came essentially from the  hearts of those who'd suffered the most  in the past it wasn't just the wonderful  Nelson Mandela coming out of prison  after 27 years with a relationship with  his gods with the former oppressors  talking negotiating it wasn't just  Oliver Tambo the other great leader of  the ANC who influenced me so directly  who implanted these ideas that we won't  have Nurembe